module bindings.c.dest_capacity_unchecked_copy;

// strcpy(3) and strcat(3) copy without any bound, so a function that writes
// one of them into a caller-supplied buffer is safe only by knowing the
// buffer is always large enough for what it copies. The one place that
// knowledge is stated is the capacity the caller hands over separately, and
// a function that already relies on it -- zeroing the buffer with
// memset(dst, c, n), filling it with fgets(dst, n, s) or fread(dst, sz, n, s),
// formatting into it with snprintf(dst, n, fmt), bounding another copy with
// strncpy(dst, src, n) -- has declared that parameter to be exactly that
// destination's byte count. An unbounded copy in the same function that never
// relates any length to that parameter copies by its own internal limit
// instead, and overflows the caller's buffer whenever the internal buffer is
// the larger of the two.
//
// The fact is emitted by the C frontend (ccDestCapacityUncheckedObservations
// and ccDestCapacityUncheckedCursorPairObservations,
// internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go) from the syntax tree: the
// capacity join and the guard scan both run over the compacted function body,
// so prose and identifiers unrelated to the pairing cannot satisfy them. The
// guard is semantic, not a spelling: any relational comparison naming the
// capacity parameter clears it, so the clamp the fix introduces
// (`if (j >= lineSize) j = lineSize-1;`), an early return, and the mirrored
// operand orders all read as consulting the capacity.
//
// The second frontend fact pairs a capacity the code states itself rather than
// one the caller hands over: a walk that advances a destination field and
// decrements a same-object counter field by one length, in one block, has
// declared that counter to be the destination's remaining byte count, so a
// transfer of that length whose bound never mentions the counter -- a length
// taken from the source side, or clamped by a constant -- is bounded by the
// source and not by the destination. The counter must be the one counter of
// that object the length is charged to, and the counter reaching the transfer
// call, or entering the length's own assignment, reads as consulting it just
// as a comparison does.
//
// Residuals, all false-negative: a comparison on either side of a branch
// boundary clears every copy in the function, so a clamp that bounds one
// branch's copy also vouches for a sibling branch's; the compacted body keeps
// comments, so a commented-out capacity check still clears; and a wrapper
// around a capacity-taking libc call (a project's own prefixed spelling) is
// not paired, so only the standard names identify the capacity. The cursor
// form adds that the destination and its length must reach the transfer call
// as bare arguments, so a cast around either leaves the copy unpaired.
binding destCapacityUncheckedCopy {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "any" and node.analysis == "copy.dest_capacity_unchecked"
  emit issue code.DestCapacityUncheckedCopy at node
}
